<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Da Yining</h1><p class="st-meta">929 CE – 937 CE · capital Yangjumie</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short dynasty of Yang Ganzhen in western Yunnan, overthrown in 937 by Duan Siping, founder of the Dali Kingdom.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-ganzhen">Yang Ganzhen</a>, a leading official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-erhai">Erhai</a> region who had brought down <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/da-changhe">Da Changhe</a> in 928 and installed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/da-tianxing">Da Tianxing</a>, deposed his own puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-shanzheng">Zhao Shanzheng</a> in 929 and proclaimed the kingdom of Da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yining">Yining</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangjumie">Yangjumie</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sources are scanty. Later accounts describe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-ganzhen">Yang Ganzhen's</a> rule as oppressive and his government as unstable, alienating both the Bai nobility and the many tribal peoples of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-siping">Duan Siping</a>, a military commander of the Tonghai region, allied with eastern tribes, marched on the capital and overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-ganzhen">Yang Ganzhen</a>. He founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a>, which lasted until the Mongol conquest.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 929 CE – 937 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.19%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>929 CE – 937 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yangjumie</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yang Ganzhen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bai, Middle Chinese for writing</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dayining</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=933&amp;focus=da-yining" aria-label="Da Yining on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7416 1004 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Da Yining lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7654 1490h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7736 1260h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7736 1260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=933&amp;focus=da-yining">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
